    "teaserText": "A sword is a fine weapon. A spear is a fine reason to keep your distance.",
    "startText": "News arrived that Rome would soon face Greek forces. \n\n[angry drunk] \"Never bring a sword to a spear fight unless you have a very good plan for getting close.\"\n\nAnthony examined his gladius in his big hand.\n\n\"Could I run?\"\n\n[angry drunk]\"You could,\" said the instructor. [jokingly drunk]\"But a Roman with a sword running away from a Greek with a spear, is a poor opening to any story.\"\n\n[stern] \"Remember, lads! A spear has the advantage of reaching you before you reach its owner. Keep your distance, watch for an opening and don't mistake courage for stupidity. The graveyards are full of brave men.\"",
    "winText": "[angry, slightly drunk] \"Good! A man who knows how he might [strong emphasis]die[/strong emphasis] will take every step to avoid it!\"\n\nAnthony considered this the finest military wisdom he had ever heard. He only wished someone had told him before he joined the army.",
    "loseText": "The spear reaches farther than the gladius. Charging straight at a hoplite is an excellent way to become an example in someone else's training lesson. Watch your distance, approach carefully and strike when the opening comes.",
